the league.","offense":"The Raiders offense was well below average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 2nd highest under center rate and the 2nd highest under center rate in the league. Aidan O'Connell ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Davante Adams, who drew 33% of the targets.","defense":"The Raiders defense was well above average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was EPA allowed per play, where it ranked 10th, and its weakest was pressure rate, where it ranked 20th. Maxx Crosby was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Malcolm Koonce, who had 8 sacks while playing only 44% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the highest Cover 2 rate in the league.","special":"The Raiders were one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 25% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers made 87% of field goals, which was a little above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 22% of the money on the books. The offense also turned over more of its roster than most, with 42% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Aidan O'Connell played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,218 yards. Davante Adams was the clear top target with 33% of the looks and 1,144 receiving yards. Josh Jacobs handled most of the work in the backfield with 62% of the carries. On defense, Maxx Crosby led the team with 14.5 sacks and Tre'von Moehrig picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Texans under Bobby Slowik and the 2025 Vikings under Kevin O'Connell. Their defense looked most like the 2024 Colts and the 2022 Texans.","trends":"The offense went from 17th in EPA per play in 2022 to 25th in 2023, a big step back. The defense moved from 29th in EPA allowed per play in 2022 to 10th in 2023, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was Cover 1 rate, which went from 31% to 23%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2023. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}